"Annihilation of slowly-decaying terms of Navier–Stokes flows by external forcing (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/349923639_Annihilation_of_slowly-decaying_terms_of_Navier-Stokes_flows_by_external_forcing)\nciteturn2search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.6 years ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; Lorenzo Brandolese ... The goal of this paper is to provide an algorithm that, for any sufficiently localised, divergence-free small initial data, explicitly constructs a localised external force leading to a rapidly dissipative solutions of the Navier–Stokes equations Rn : namely, the energy decay rate of the flow will be forced to satisfy ‖u(t)‖22=o(t−(n+2)/2) as t → ∞, which is beyond the usual optimal rate.\n\n# Annihilation of slowly-decaying terms of Navier–Stokes flows by external forcing\n\nNonlinearity\n\n  * March 2021\n  * 34(3):1733-1757\n\nDOI:10.1088/1361-6544/abdbbf\n\nAuthors:\n\nLorenzo Brandolese\n\nLorenzo Brandolese\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nTakahiro Okabe\n\nTakahiro Okabe\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe goal of this paper is to provide an algorithm that, for any sufficiently localised, divergence-free small initial data, explicitly constructs a localised external force leading to a rapidly dissipative solutions of the Navier–Stokes equations Rn : namely, the energy decay rate of the flow will be forced to satisfy ‖u(t)‖22=o(t−(n+2)/2) as t → ∞, which is beyond the usual optimal rate. An important feature of our construction is that this force can always be taken compactly supported in space–time, and its profile arbitrarily prescribed up to a spatial rescaling. Since the forcing term vanishes after a finite time interval, our result suggests that nontrivial interactions between the linear and nonlinear parts occur, annihilating all the slowly decaying terms contained in Miyakawa and Schonbek’s asymptotic profiles.\n\n\nLondon Mathematical Society Nonlinearity\n\nNonlinearity 34 (2021) 1733–1757 https://doi.org/10.1088/1361-6544/abdbbf\n\nAnnihilation of slowly-decaying terms of\n\nNavier–Stokes flows by external forcing\n\nLorenzo Brandolese 1,∗and Takahiro Okabe 2\n\n1 Institut Camille Jordan, Universit´\n\ne Lyon 1 Lyon, France\n\n2 Graduate School of Engineering Science, Osaka University, Osaka, Japan\n\nE-mail: brandolese@math.univ-lyon1.fr and okabe@sigmath.es.osaka-u.ac.jp\n\nReceived 25 August 2020, revised 5 January 2021\n\nAccepted for publication 14 January 2021\n\nPublished 2 March 2021\n\nAbstract\n\nThe goal of this paper is to provide an algorithm that, for any sufciently\n\nlocalised, divergence-free small initial d ata, explicitly constructs a localised\n\nexternal force leading to a rapidly dissipative solutions of the Navier–Stokes\n\nequations R n: namely, the energy decay rate of the ow will be forced to sat-\n\nisfy u(t)2\n\n2=o(t−(n+2)/2)as t→∞, which is beyond the usual optimal rate.\n\nAn important feature of our construction is that this force can always be taken\n\ncompactly supported in space–time, and its prole arbitrarily prescribed up to\n\na spatial rescaling. Since the forcing term vanishes after a nite time interval,\n\nour result suggests that nontrivial interactions between the linear and nonlinear\n\nparts occur, annihilating all the slowl y decaying terms contained in Miyakawa\n\nand Schonbek’s asymptotic proles.\n\nKeywords: asymptotic proles, fast dissipation, energy decay\n\nMathematics Subject Classication numbers: 35Q30, 76D05.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nbrandolese_definitivo.dvi (https://ems.press/content/serial-article-files/38087)\nciteturn2search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 22.5 years ago; Lorenzo Brandolese ... We study the large time behavior of the energy of a class of Navier– ... ables, of weak and strong solutions to the Navier–Stokes system. ... the pressure.\nRev. Mat. Iberoamericana 20 (2004), 223–256\nAsymptotic behavior of the energy\nand pointwise estimates for solutions\nto the Navier-Stokes equations\nLorenzo Brandolese\nAbstract\nWe study the large time behavior of the energy of a class of Navier–\nStokes ﬂows in Rn (n ≥2) with special symmetries. Inside this class,\nwe construct examples of solutions such that the energy norm decays\nfaster than t−(n+2)/4.\n1. Introduction\nIn this paper we deal with the asymptotic behavior, in the space-time vari-\nables, of weak and strong solutions to the Navier–Stokes system. For an\nincompressible viscous ﬂuid which ﬁlls the whole space Rn, in the absence\nof external forces, the Navier–Stokes equations read\n\n\n(NS)\n\n∂tu + ∇· (u ⊗u) = ∆u −∇p\nu(x, 0) = a(x)\ndiv(u) = 0.\nHere u : Rn × [0, ∞[→Rn (n ≥2) denotes the velocity ﬁeld and p(x, t) is\nthe pressure.\nStarting with the pioneering work of Leray [21], a considerable number\nof papers is concerned with questions related to the large-time behavior of\nthe L2-norm of u(t). The problem of ﬁnding optimal decay rates for the\nenergy of generic weak solutions is now well understood. Indeed, Wiegner\n[41] showed that ||u(t)||2 ≤C(1 + t)−α (0 < α ≤(n + 2)/4), if such decay\nholds for the solution et∆a of the heat equation starting with the same data.\n2000 Mathematics Subject Classiﬁcation: 76D05, 35Q30, 35B40, 42C40.\nKeywords: Navier–Stokes equations, time decay, space decay, symmetric solutions.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNew asymptotic profiles of nonstationary solutions of the Navier–Stokes system - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0021782407000529)\nciteturn2search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Author links open overlay panel Lorenzo Brandolese ^{a}, François Vigneron ^{b} ... We show that solutions of the nonstationary incompressible Navier–Stokes system in {\\mathbb{R}}^{d} () starting from mild decaying data a behave as as a potential field:(i) where {\\gamma }_{d} is a constant and is the energy matrix of the flow.\n\nDate: July 2007\n\nPages: 64-86\n\nVolume: Volume 88, Issue 1\n\n## Published by: Elsevier\n\n# New asymptotic profiles of nonstationary solutions of the Navier–Stokes system\n\nAuthor links open overlay panel Lorenzo Brandolese ^{a}, François Vigneron ^{b}\n\nShow more\n\nView PDF Download full issue\n\nCite\n\nAdd to Mendeley\n\nShare\n\n10.1016/j.matpur.2007.04.007\n\nMore actions\n\n  * Article\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe show that solutions of the nonstationary incompressible Navier–Stokes system in {\\mathbb{R}}^{d} () starting from mild decaying data a behave as as a potential field:(i) where {\\gamma }_{d} is a constant and is the energy matrix of the flow.\n\nWe deduce that, for well localized data, and for small t and large enough ,(ii) where the lower bound holds on the complementary of a set of directions, of arbitrary small measure on {\\mathbb{S}}^{d-1}. We also obtain new lower bounds for the large time decay of the weighted-{L}^{p} norms, extending previous results of Schonbek, Miyakawa, Bae and Jin.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAsymptotic behavior of the energy and pointwise estimates for solutions to the Navier-Stokes equations | EMS Press (https://ems.press/journals/rmi/articles/4953)\nciteturn2search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 22.5 years ago; Crawled: last week; Lorenzo Brandolese, Asymptotic behavior of the energy and pointwise estimates for solutions to the Navier-Stokes equations.\n\n# Asymptotic behavior of the energy and pointwise estimates for solutions to the Navier-Stokes equations\n\n  * ### Lorenzo Brandolese\n\nUniversité Lyon 1, Villeurbanne, France\n    * zbMATH\n\nImage: Asymptotic behavior of the energy and pointwise estimates for solutions to the Navier-Stokes equations cover\n\nDownload PDF\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe study the large time behavior of the energy of a class of Navier-Stokes flows in R n (n≥2) with special symmetries. Inside this class, we construct examples of solutions such that the energy norm decays faster than t−(n+2)/4.\n\n## Cite this article\n\nLorenzo Brandolese, Asymptotic behavior of the energy and pointwise estimates for solutions to the Navier-Stokes equations. Rev. Mat. Iberoam. 20 (2004), no. 1, pp. 223–256\n\nDOI 10.4171/RMI/387\n\n## Dates\n\nSubmitted\n    9 May 2002\nPublished\n    30 April 2004\n\n## Identifiers\n\nDOI\n    10.4171/RMI/387\n\n## Keywords\n\n  * Navier–Stokes equations\n  * time decay\n  * space decay\n  * symmetric solutions\n\n## Mathematics Subject Classification\n\n  * 76-XX\n  * 35-XX\n  * 42-XX\n  * 00-XX--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nForced rapidly dissipative Navier–Stokes ﬂows (https://borowiecki.dev/pdf/2502.12733)\nciteturn2search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.6 years ago; Lorenzo Brandolese ... we can increase the energy dissipation rate of any small solution of the Navier–Stokes equations in ... Its support can be chosen to be contained in an arbitrarily small region, in time or in space. ... scalar ﬁeld π is the pressure and f = f(x, t) is a n × n matrix-valued function. ... Contrary to Navier–Stokes ﬂows with the usual energy dissipation rate O(t−(n+2)/2), such rapidly dis-\nForced rapidly dissipative Navier–Stokes ﬂows\nTakahiro Okabe\nGraduate School of Engineering Science\nOsaka University\nE-mail: okabe@sigmath.es.osaka-u.ac.jp\nLorenzo Brandolese\nInstitut Camille Jordan\nUniversit´e Lyon 1\nE-mail: brandolese@math.univ-lyon1.fr\nAbstract\nWe show that, by acting on a ﬁnite number of parameters of a compactly supported control force,\nwe can increase the energy dissipation rate of any small solution of the Navier–Stokes equations in\nRn. The magnitude of the control force is bounded by a negative Sobolev norm of the initial velocity.\nIts support can be chosen to be contained in an arbitrarily small region, in time or in space.\nKey word: Navier-Stokes equations, Energy decay, Asymptotic proﬁles, Control force.\nMSC(2010): 35Q30; 76D05\n1\nIntroduction\nLet n ≥2. We consider the incompressible Navier-Stokes equations in Rn:\n\n\n\n(NS)\n∂tu −∆u + u · ∇u + ∇π = ∇· f\nin Rn × (0, ∞),\ndiv u = 0\nin Rn × (0, ∞),\n\n\nu(·, 0) = a\nin Rn,\nHere a = (a1, . . . , an) and u = (u1, . . . , un)(·, t) are the velocity ﬁeld at the time 0 and t respectively, the\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn the instantaneous spreading for the Navier-Stokes system in the whole space (https://www.numdam.org/item/?id=COCV_2002__8__273_0)\nciteturn2search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 24.8 years ago; Crawled: last week; Brandolese, Lorenzo ^{} ; Meyer, Yves ^{} ... Brandolese, On the Localization of Symmetric and Asymmetric Solutions of the Navier-Stokes Equations dans {ℝ}^{n}.\n\nOn the instantaneous spreading for the Navier-Stokes system in the whole space\n\nBrandolese, Lorenzo ^{} ; Meyer, Yves ^{}\n\nESAIM: Control, Optimisation and Calculus of Variations, Tome 8 (2002), pp. 273-285\n\nWe consider the spatial behavior of the velocity field u(x,t) of a fluid filling the whole space {ℝ}^{n} (n\\ge 2) for arbitrarily small values of the time variable. We improve previous results on the spatial spreading by deducing the necessary conditions \\int {u}_{h}(x,t){u}_{k}(x,t)\\phantom{\\rule{0.166667em}{0ex}}\\mathrm{d}x=c\\left(t\\right){\\delta }_{h,k} under more general assumptions on the localization of u. We also give some new examples of solutions which have a stronger spatial localization than in the generic case.\n\nZbl\n\nDOI : 10.1051/cocv:2002021\n\nClassification : 35B40, 76D05, 35Q30\nKeywords: Navier-Stokes equations, space-decay, symmetries\n    \n    @article{COCV_2002__8__273_0,\n         author = {Brandolese, Lorenzo and Meyer, Yves},\n         title = {On the instantaneous spreading for the {Navier-Stokes} system in the whole space},\n         journal